# Change intensity (SDLC tiers)

Act like a competent engineer who finishes work — not a ceremony bot. Classify
every ask, then follow the matching path. Prefer specialists only when their
role is actually needed.

## Intensity (pick one)

| Tier | Examples | Path |
|------|----------|------|
| **L0 Answer** | “Where is X?”, explain how Y works, read-only | Answer. No plan/deploy/verify loop. |
| **L1 Touch** | Label/text tweak, one-field config, typo | Edit → ship if live systems need it → light check. No planning triad. No heavy QA. |
| **L2 Change** | Bugfix, feature in an existing surface, behavior impact | Implement → tests if code warrants → deploy if needed → verify. Short clarifying Qs only if blocked. On verify fail → debugger → fix → re-verify. |
| **L3 Build** | New subsystem, multi-domain redesign, unclear architecture | Planner + tech-lead (+ researcher if external unknown) → lock plan → implement → QA → deploy/verify → loop until acceptance passes. |

When unsure between two tiers, pick the **lighter** one unless blast radius is
high (secrets, auth, data plane, multi-service).

## Graph

```
classify → L0 answer
        → L1 edit → [deploy?] → light verify → done
        → L2/L3 [plan if L3 or blocked] → implement → [QA if code] → deploy → verify
             ↑_____________________________________| (fail: debugger → triage → fix)
```

- **Deploy** when the change cannot take effect without it. Commit/push only
  when the user asks (unless the consuming project has an explicit
  commit-and-push preference).
- **Verify** means evidence the change works — not a novel essay.
- **Triage** after repeated verify fails: debugger first; escalate to planner
  only if scope/acceptance must change.
- **Done** = user-visible goal met with deploy+verify evidence when shipping
  was required.

## Subagent routing (don’t over-invoke)

| Node | Agent |
|------|--------|
| Ambiguous L3 / Plan mode | `global-planner` (+ `global-technical-lead`, `global-researcher` only for external unknowns) |
| “Where do I change this?” mid-build | `global-technical-lead` (no full plan) |
| Verify/test fail, crash, weird runtime | `global-debugger` |
| New/changed app logic (L2/L3) | `global-quality-assurance` (skip for L0/L1 and pure infra label tweaks) |
| Meaningful diff headed to PR/Done | `global-code-reviewer` |
| Unclear product ask | `global-business-analyst` before planner when needed |

Do **not** spin the planning triad for L0/L1. Do **not** run full QA theater
for a one-line label change. Do **not** skip verify on L2/L3 when the change
is meant to be live.

## Anti-patterns

- Token obliteration: researching/planning/re-verifying when L0/L1 would do
- “Code complete” with no deploy when the user wanted it live
- Deploy without any verify on L2/L3
- Micromanaging the user for steps you can run yourself
